---
// §9.7 Try It Yourself — plain-language (Krug/Redish), menu-first, teaser not a full
// copy of the README. Links out to the repo rather than duplicating setup steps.
// Human review (2026-07-23) added: a Docker Desktop download link + InfoTip
// (short glossary popover), a git-free "Download ZIP" path with BeginnersTrail
// guidance for readers who don't know what GitHub/cloning/a terminal are, and a
// reworded "easy way" intro that no longer assumes "cloning" was already explained.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import InfoTip from '../components/InfoTip.astro';
import BeginnersTrail from '../components/BeginnersTrail.astro';
import { REPO_URL, withBase } from '../lib/site';

const ZIP_URL = `${REPO_URL}/archive/refs/heads/main.zip`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Run it yourself — Preempt Analytics"
  description="Run the Preempt Analytics predictive-maintenance demo on your own machine. One prerequisite (Docker Desktop), a friendly menu, and a link to the full instructions on GitHub."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 py-16 md:px-8 md:py-24">
    <a href={withBase('/')} class="text-sm text-teal-400 hover:text-teal-300">← Back to home</a>

    <h1 class="mt-6 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-offwhite sm:text-5xl">
      Run Preempt Analytics on your own machine.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
      Everything runs inside Docker — no Python, no ML knowledge needed. Just
      one prerequisite and a couple of minutes.
    </p>

    <BeginnersTrail title="Never done anything like this before? Here's the whole path.">
      <p>You don't need to know how to code. The whole journey is three steps:</p>
      <p>
        <strong>1. Install Docker Desktop</strong> (below) — one app, like installing
        any other program.<br />
        <strong>2. Get the code onto your computer</strong> — click a
        "Download ZIP" button, no typing required.<br />
        <strong>3. Run the menu script</strong> and press a number — it starts
        everything for you.
      </p>
      <p>That's the whole thing. Nothing below requires typing commands unless you want to.</p>
    </BeginnersTrail>

    <!-- Prerequisite -->
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-navy-800/40 p-5">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-offwhite">
        You need one thing:
        <a
          href="https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener"
          class="text-teal-300 underline decoration-teal-400/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-teal-200"
        >
          Docker Desktop
        </a>
        <InfoTip label="What is Docker Desktop?">
          Docker Desktop is a free app that runs this whole project in a
          self-contained box on your computer — you won't need to separately
          install Python, databases, or anything else by hand.
        </InfoTip>
      </p>
      <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-muted">
        Install it and make sure it's running (the whale icon in your menu/task bar).
        Nothing else to set up.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Get the code: git-free ZIP path first, git second -->
    <h2 class="mt-16 text-xl font-semibold text-offwhite">Get the code onto your computer</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">Two ways to do this — pick whichever feels easier.</p>
    <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
      <a
        href={ZIP_URL}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg bg-teal-400 px-5 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-navy-950 transition-colors hover:bg-teal-300"
      >
        Download ZIP (no git needed) ↓
      </a>
      <a href={REPO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-sm text-teal-400 hover:text-teal-300">
        or view the source on GitHub →
      </a>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
      Unzip it anywhere — that folder is your project folder for everything
      below. Using git instead? The full clone command is further down this
      page.
    </p>

    <BeginnersTrail title="What's GitHub? What does &ldquo;cloning&rdquo; mean?">
      <p>
        <strong>GitHub</strong> is a website where code is stored and shared —
        think of it like a shared drive, but for software projects.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Cloning</strong> just means downloading a full copy of that
        project onto your own computer. The "Download ZIP" button above does
        the same job without needing git installed at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        A <strong>terminal</strong> (or "command line") is a text-based way of
        giving your computer instructions. If that sounds intimidating, you
        don't need one — nothing on this page beyond double-clicking a file
        requires typing a command.
      </p>
    </BeginnersTrail>

    <!-- Friendly path first -->
    <h2 class="mt-16 text-xl font-semibold text-offwhite">The easy way — a menu, no commands to remember</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      Once you have the project folder on your computer (see above), run one
      script. It starts everything for you — you won't need to run any Docker
      commands yourself. Then press a number to simulate sensor readings, open
      the drift report, or watch the pipeline retrain itself.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-navy-950 p-4">
      <pre class="text-sm text-teal-200"><code># Mac / Linux
./preempt.sh

# Windows
.\preempt.ps1</code></pre>
    </div>

    <!-- Manual path second -->
    <h2 class="mt-16 text-xl font-semibold text-offwhite">Prefer to type it? Three commands</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
      This replaces everything above — get the code and start the stack in
      one go, no menu needed.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-navy-950 p-4">
      <pre class="text-sm text-teal-200"><code>git clone https://github.com/Preempt-Analytics-Demo/predictive-maintenance-demo.git
cd predictive-maintenance-demo
docker compose up -d</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
      From there you can generate sensor readings and watch drift detection trigger
      an automatic retraining run — the full walkthrough lives in the README.
    </p>

    <!-- Shared-resource expectation -->
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-teal-400/20 bg-teal-400/[0.04] p-5">
      <p class="text-sm text-muted">
        <span class="font-semibold text-teal-300">Heads up:</span> retraining runs on a
        shared demo pipeline (about 10 runs/hour for everyone combined). If it's busy,
        your data still uploads and your run simply queues — nothing is broken.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12">
      <a
        href={REPO_URL}
        rel="noopener"
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg bg-teal-400 px-6 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-navy-950 transition-colors hover:bg-teal-300"
      >
        Full instructions on GitHub →
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
